# Knockout Mode

> Scope: frontend flow, runtime mode translation, race options, and lap-based
> elimination behavior for the custom Knockout mode.

## Frontend Flow

Ghidra MCP confirmed that the Start Race frontend menu is built by
`BuildStartRaceMenu` at `0x0055e730` and handled by
`HandleStartRaceMenuAction` at `0x0055e880`.

The native menu registers these entries:

- Single Race
- Championship
- Multiplayer
- Time Trial
- Practice
- Stunt Arena
- Clockwork Carnage, when unlocked
- developer-only modes, when dev/unlock settings allow them

`HandleStartRaceMenuAction` switches on the selected menu item label id. The
Single Race row uses label id `0x15`; choosing it sets `GameModeRuntime::mode`
to `MODE_SINGLE_RACE` (`2`) and copies the random race flags from the frontend
settings block at `RaceSettings + 0xA4` into `GameMode + 0x2E`.

After that, native frontend flow continues through the same screens requested
for Knockout:

1. `BuildDifficultySelectionMenu` (`0x005552d0`) for Simulation / Arcade /
   Console / Junior RC.
2. `BuildPlayerNameInputMenu` (`0x0055ec50`) for the name wheel.
3. `BuildCarSelectionMenu` (`0x00557740`) for car selection.
4. `BuildTrackSelectionMenu` (`0x0055c390`) for track selection.
5. `BuildPreRaceSummaryMenu` (`0x00555670`) when the native flow requests it,
   otherwise direct launch from the track selector.

Knockout reuses this path instead of adding a new native `GameMode` value. The
DLL adds a custom Knockout descriptor to the Start Race menu by cloning the
runtime Single Race descriptor after the native builder runs, then the handler
sets the native mode to `MODE_SINGLE_RACE` while keeping separate
randomizer-side Knockout selection flags. `modeActive` persists across in-race
restarts, while `menuSelectionActive` records the frontend selection path.

This avoids patching the `RaceSessionSetup` native mode jump table at
`0x00450700`, whose known cases only cover existing RVGL modes.

## Game Settings

`RegisterMenuItemInActiveMenu` at `0x005453b0` is hooked so the DLL can detect
when `BuildSettingsMenu` registers the native `Num Cars` row. The two Knockout
numeric entries are then registered immediately after that row in the Game
Settings menu:

- `Knockout Lap Frequency`: `1..10`, measured in laps.
- `Knockout Eliminations Per Lap`: `1..29`, number of cars eliminated per event.

The values live in `RandomizerContext::knockoutState`. They are runtime menu
settings and are not currently persisted to RVGL's INI.

## Race Runtime

`RaceSessionSetup` starts a Knockout race only when the persistent mode flag
is active and the native mode is Single Race. This means existing Single Race
participant generation, 30-car expansion, grid handling, and leaderboard
fallbacks still apply.

`Engine_UpdateRaceProgress` at `0x005257e0` increments each car's completed lap
counter at `CarEntity + 0x0F40` after `UpdateCarRoutePosition` reports a lap
crossing. The Knockout pass runs after the native function and reads those
completed lap counts.

Base elimination logic:

- For one car per event, when `activeCars - 1` cars have completed the target
  lap, the remaining car is eliminated.
- For multiple cars per event, when `activeCars - eliminationsPerEvent` cars
  have completed the target lap, the remaining lowest-position cars are
  eliminated.
- The next target lap advances by `eliminationFrequencyLaps`.
- The event never eliminates the final remaining car.

Eliminated cars are added to the native 30-entry race result table through
`RegisterFinishTime` at `0x004f0890` with the DNF flag set, using the live race
clock from `PTR_g_CurrentRaceClockMS`. Knockout then rewrites the native result
table so eliminated cars occupy the back positions in elimination order while
still displaying the real knockout time. The first eliminated car is therefore
shown as the last-place result, the second eliminated car as second-to-last, and
so on.

`RegisterFinishTime` does not display the normal finish popup for DNF entries,
so Knockout calls `UI_SetPostRacePopup` at `0x00468200` directly with the same
position/name/time format used by native finish messages. When one event
eliminates multiple cars, Knockout uses its own multi-line popup renderer so
each row is centered independently and the block grows upward instead of
downward from the native one-line anchor. Multi-line knockout popups show only
position/name rows, colored with each eliminated car's native popup color. Rows
are sorted by displayed finish position, with ranks right-aligned so names share
the same starting column.

Eliminated cars are moved through native `SetCarBehaviourState` at `0x004efd50`
instead of directly assigning `CarEntity::nCar_type`. Ghidra MCP showed that
state `5` is not collisionless: it still sets `PhysicsEntity::nCollision_mode`
to `2` and uses the normal car collision callback at `0x004f0cf0`. State `4`
uses `nCollision_mode = 0` with a passive collision callback, so Knockout uses
state `4` for eliminated cars and restores state `1` / `3` for player / CPU
cars during knockout race cleanup. State `4` can move the car to native ghost
metadata positions through its primary update callback. The callback pointer is
loaded from `PTR_FUN_006a4800`, resolves to `0x00523fb0`, and calls
`0x0044d560`, which reads ghost/replay frame data and writes the physics body
position. Knockout captures the live physics position/orientation before the
state switch, reapplies it once through `setCarTransform`, then clears
`PhysicsEntity::pPrimary_update` at `+0x340` and the saved primary update at
`+0x458` so native code cannot keep replaying the ghost pose. Because the car
already has a finish time, it no longer counts as actively racing. The last
remaining car is registered as the winner with a non-DNF finish time so native
race-complete handling can take over.

When a Knockout race is reset or restarted, Knockout clears its cached results,
the native race result table, each live car's finish time, and each live car's
completed-lap counter. Cars left in the Knockout ghost state are restored to
the normal player/CPU race states before the next race starts. Cleanup runs both
around `RaceSessionSetup` and after car setup/grid placement, and the update
hook also detects the race clock moving backwards as a defensive mid-race
restart signal.

Native leaderboard drawing compacts non-empty result entries toward the top, so
Knockout replaces `DrawPostRaceLeaderboard` while Knockout results exist. The
custom renderer reserves one visual row per participant and draws each
eliminated car at its real result index, so the first eliminated car appears on
the bottom row. It only draws while the native post-race result table would be
visible and suppresses itself when a frontend menu panel is active, such as the
pause menu.

Because the native flow still stores `MODE_SINGLE_RACE`, Knockout temporarily
patches the native Single Race locale label to `Knockout` after the custom mode
is selected. This lets the pre-race summary and loading screen show the custom
mode name. The original Single Race label is restored when the Start Race menu
is rebuilt or when another Start Race mode is confirmed.

## Current Risks

- The custom frontend labels currently patch existing locale table slots at
  runtime. This is temporary; a safer custom-label strategy is still needed.
- Knockout is implemented as a Single Race subtype, so native profile progress
  and result screens still see `MODE_SINGLE_RACE`.
- The options are not persisted outside the current process.
